## Return for direction (mandatory triggers)

Stop and return NEED-DIRECTION when (an attempt is one edit-plus-check cycle): the same
goal fails 3 attempts; the same error survives 2 attempts unchanged (whichever of these
two comes first); there is a route choice the list did not make; something needed
is missing after one search; a known-answer check still fails after 1 fix; a resource limit
trips; a result contradicts this handoff or the records; or you are about to write a
judgment of difficulty or scope.

```
NEED-DIRECTION (<this handoff>, brick <k>)
TRIGGER: <which, and the exact symptom>
STATE: <files, last error, last output>
TRIED: <one line per attempt, saying where it broke>
ROUTES I SEE: <options; do NOT pick one>
EVERYTHING ELSE LANDED: <yes, or what is open>
CLAIM: <proof or construction bricks only: NOT-PROVED | NONE (escalated: <trigger>)>
```

A `DONE` on a proof or construction brick adds `CLAIM: PROVED | COUNTEREXAMPLE`; a `PARTIAL`
adds `CLAIM: GAPS (core: <list>; routine: <list>)` or, for an argument that convinces but
has a non-deductive step or an open premise, `CLAIM: ARGUED (<labels>; premises: <list>)` with
the short form in the worker charter; and its `TRIED:` lines. Attempt first; abstain after,
never instead. A `DONE` that establishes a result, or a brick where a structure emerged (a closed
form, a recurrence, a pattern), adds `FRAMING: <what it resembles or may be known as:
candidate names to search, or none>`; the coordinator searches them before building on it.

